{"reference":"Ruth 1","book":"Ruth","chapter":1,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"archaeology_geography","title":"Places and movement","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"The chapter begins in Bethlehem in Judah, with Judah named as the setting. Famine sends Elimelech’s family from Bethlehem to Moab, and the chapter closes with Naomi and Ruth returning to Bethlehem. These place names frame the chapter’s movement from departure to return without supplying an itinerary between the two places.","verse_refs":["Ruth 1:1-2","Ruth 1:19-22"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_ruth_001_a87c7e03071e33fd8da29908","reader_path_ruth_001_504abb4b5ff182dcf2887036","reader_path_ruth_001_a0020b0b024377bc9ed4b7fc"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"fact"}]},{"kind":"historical_context","title":"Ruth’s pledge","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"Ruth’s response to Naomi is more than an agreement to travel together. She pledges to go and live where Naomi does, to identify with Naomi’s people and God, and to remain with Naomi until death. The scene therefore presents Ruth’s decision as a comprehensive, enduring commitment after Orpah has returned home.","verse_refs":["Ruth 1:16-18"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_ruth_001_ba143a52c924176dd3d46f0e"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"fact"}]},{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"A debated question","blocks":[{"id":"block_3","text":"The narrative continues to call Ruth “the Moabitess” even as she commits herself to Naomi, Naomi’s people, and Naomi’s God. Some readers connect this feature to later debates about intermarriage, but the proposal that Ruth was written as a direct response to a particular later policy remains debated.","verse_refs":["Ruth 1:1","Ruth 1:6","Ruth 1:16-18"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_ruth_001_3c0572b2558deeb811c683b5"],"confidence":"low","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"The wider story","blocks":[{"id":"block_4","text":"Ruth 1 opens the book’s larger movement: famine, bereavement, and Naomi’s description of herself as empty are followed by harvest, kinship redemption, birth, communal blessing, and genealogy. The arrival at the beginning of barley harvest is therefore the opening turn within that broader movement, not the story’s resolution.","verse_refs":["Ruth 2:1-23","Ruth 3:1-18","Ruth 4:1-22"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_ruth_001_b5bf7ef03c21537b1fe1dbfe"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"fact"},{"id":"block_5","text":"A later canonical discussion in Nehemiah 13 applies an exclusion concerning Ammonites and Moabites while addressing Tobiah’s access to temple space. Read alongside Ruth the Moabite and texts that welcome faithful foreigners, this material should not be turned into a universal ethnic ban; how these texts relate is a debated interpretive question.","verse_refs":["Ruth 2:1-23","Ruth 3:1-18","Ruth 4:1-22"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_ruth_001_1ea3e942e71e422247c79172"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}